":["Vulture"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Vultur is a genus of New World vulture that contains two species, an extant species, the Andean condor (Vultur gryphus) and the fossil species Vultur messii from the early Pliocene of Argentina.","revisionId":1311783206,"title":"Vultur","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vultur","wikidataId":"Q15042113"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Echinoderms","source":"Martha's Vineyard species checklist"},{"language":"en","name":"Brittle-stars, Sea Urchins, Starfish Etc","source":"Martha's Vineyard species checklist"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Echinodermata","confidence":94,"rank":"phylum","scientificName":"Echinodermata","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":50},"ottId":451020,"rank":"phylum","scientificName":"Echinodermata","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"An echinoderm () is any animal of the phylum Echinodermata (), which includes starfish, brittle stars, sea urchins, sand dollars and sea cucumbers, as well as the sessile sea lilies or \"stone lilies\". While bilaterally symmetrical as larvae, as adults echinoderms are recognisable by their usually five-pointed radial symmetry (pentamerous symmetry), and are found on the sea bed at every ocean depth from the intertidal zone to the abyssal zone.","revisionId":1369613656,"title":"Echinoderm","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Echinoderm","wikidataId":"Q44631"}},{"commonNames":[],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Piprites","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Piprites Cabanis, 1847","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2486425},"ottId":456268,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Piprites","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Piprites is a genus of bird currently placed in the family Tyrannidae. Prior to 1971, the genus was placed in the family Pipridae; its designation was initially changed based on morphological evidence, and genetic evidence confirmed its placement in 2009.","revisionId":1364287603,"title":"Piprites","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piprites","wikidataId":"Q1059797"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Black-billed Turaco","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"BBTU","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Tauraco schuettii","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Tauraco schuettii (Cabanis, 1879)","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2475255},"ottId":487308,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Tauraco schuettii","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The black-billed turaco (Tauraco schuettii) is a medium-sized turaco, an endemic family to sub-Saharan Africa. It is a resident breeder in the forests of central Africa, found in the Democratic Republic of Congo, Uganda, West Kenya, Burundi, Rwanda and South Sudan.","revisionId":1313960517,"title":"Black-billed turaco","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black-billed_turaco","wikidataId":"Q998480"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Australian Brushturkey","source":"The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species"},{"language":"en","name":"Gweela","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Bush Turkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Scrub Turkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Australian Brush-turkey","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"brush turkey","source":"Phthiraptera.myspecies.info"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Alectura lathami","confidence":99,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Alectura lathami J.E.Gray, 1831","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":5229426},"ottId":570956,"rank":"species","scientificName":"Alectura lathami","synonyms":[],"wikipedia":{"extract":"The Australian brushturkey, Australian brush-turkey, or gweela (Alectura lathami), also commonly called the bush turkey, scrub turkey, or just brushturkey, is a large mound-building bird of the family Megapodiidae. It is found in eastern Australia, ranging from Far North Queensland to the south coast of New South Wales, and has also been introduced to Kangaroo Island in South Australia.","revisionId":1368301782,"title":"Australian brushturkey","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_brushturkey","wikidataId":"Q632066"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Cockatoos","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"White Cockatoos and Corellas","source":"Catalogue of Life"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Cacatua","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Cacatua Vieillot, 1817","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2479869},"ottId":619340,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Cacatua","synonyms":["Cacatura","Cacatus","Catacus","Kacadoe","Kacatua","Kakadoe"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Cacatua is a genus of cockatoos known as great cockatoos found from the Philippines, Indonesia, Papua New Guinea, and Solomon Islands to Australia. They have a primarily white plumage (in some species tinged pinkish or yellow), an expressive crest, and a black (subgenus Cacatua) or pale (subgenus Licmetis) bill.","revisionId":1372971849,"title":"Cacatua","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cacatua","wikidataId":"Q827257"}},{"commonNames":[{"language":"en","name":"Lark Seedsnipes","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Small Seedsnipes","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"Lesser Seedsnipes","source":"Catalogue of Life"},{"language":"en","name":"seedsnipe","source":"The Paleobiology Database"}],"gbif":{"canonicalName":"Thinocorus","confidence":94,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Thinocorus Eschscholtz, 1829","status":"ACCEPTED","usageKey":2480959},"ottId":628492,"rank":"genus","scientificName":"Thinocorus","synonyms":["Thinochorus","Thinocorys","Tinochorus"],"wikipedia":{"extract":"Thinocorus is a genus of seedsnipe, a South American family of small gregarious waders which have adapted to a vegetarian diet. These birds look superficially like partridges in structure and bill shape.","revisionId":1297516017,"title":"Thinocorus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thinocorus","wikidataId":"Q945732"}},{"commonNames":[{"langua